<article class="env-definition" id="01OG">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01OG">Definition <span data-tag="01OG">31.33.1</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme. Let $\mathcal{I} \subset \mathcal{O}_ X$ be a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals, and let $Z \subset X$ be the closed subscheme corresponding to $\mathcal{I}$, see Schemes, Definition <a href="/tag/01IO" data-tag="01IO">26.10.2</a>. The <em>blowing up of $X$ along $Z$</em>, or the <em>blowing up of $X$ in the ideal sheaf $\mathcal{I}$</em> is the morphism </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  b : \underline{\text{Proj}}_ X \left(\bigoplus \nolimits _{n \geq 0} \mathcal{I}^ n\right) \longrightarrow X  \]
</div>
  <p> The <em>exceptional divisor</em> of the blowup is the inverse image $b^{-1}(Z)$. Sometimes $Z$ is called the <em>center</em> of the blowup. </p>
</article>